{"name":"Pirate Coast","hex":"#dde7e2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pirate","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A criminal who plunders at sea; commonly attacking merchant vessels, though often pillaging port towns.","An armed ship or vessel that sails for the purpose of plundering other vessels."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To appropriate by piracy; to plunder at sea.","To create and/or sell an unauthorized copy of."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pirate"},{"term":"coast","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The edge of the land where it meets an ocean, sea, gulf, bay, or large lake. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","The side or edge of something."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To glide along without adding energy; to allow a vehicle to continue moving forward after disengaging the engine or ceasing to apply motive power.","To sail along a coast."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/coast"}],"entries":[{"title":"Piracy in the Persian Gulf","blocks":[{"text":"Piracy in the Persian Gulf describes the naval warfare that was prevalent until the 19th century and occurred between seafaring Arabs in Eastern Arabia and the British Empire in the Persian Gulf. It was perceived as one of the primary threats to global maritime trade routes, particularly those with significance to British India and Iraq. Many of the most notable historical instances of these raids were conducted by the Al Qasimi tribe. This led to the British mounting the Persian Gulf campaign of 1809, a major maritime action launched by the Royal Navy to bombard Ras Al Khaimah, Lingeh and other Al Qasimi ports. Some critics of the historical narrative, such as Sharjah ruler Sultan bin Muhammad Al Qasimi in his book The Myth of Piracy in the Gulf, argue that the allegations of piracy were exaggerated by the East India Company to cut off untaxed trade routes between the Middle East and India."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Strait_of_hormuz.jpg/330px-Strait_of_hormuz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piracy_in_the_Persian_Gulf","wikidata":"Q19895289"},{"title":"Piracy","blocks":[{"text":"Piracy is an act of robbery or criminal violence by ship or boat-borne attackers upon another ship or a coastal area, typically intending to steal cargo and valuable goods, or take hostages. Those who conduct acts of piracy are called pirates, and vessels used for piracy are called pirate ships. The earliest documented instances of piracy date to the 14th century BC, when the Sea Peoples, a group of ocean raiders, attacked the ships of the Aegean and Mediterranean civilizations. Narrow channels which funnel shipping into predictable routes have long created opportunities for piracy, as well as for privateering and commerce raiding."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Flag_of_Edward_England.svg/330px-Flag_of_Edward_England.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piracy","wikidata":"Q45393"},{"title":"Coast","blocks":[{"text":"A coast is the land next to the sea or the line that forms the boundary between the land and the ocean or a lake. Coasts are influenced by the topography of the surrounding landscape and by aquatic erosion, such as that caused by waves. The geological composition of rock and soil dictates the type of shore that is created. Earth has about 620,000.0 km (385,250.1 mi) of coastline."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Spring_Lake%2C_New_Jersey_Beach_at_Sunrise.jpg/330px-Spring_Lake%2C_New_Jersey_Beach_at_Sunrise.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coast","wikidata":"Q93352"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/PersianGulf_vue_satellite_du_golfe_persique.jpg/500px-PersianGulf_vue_satellite_du_golfe_persique.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/PersianGulf_vue_satellite_du_golfe_persique.jpg/1280px-PersianGulf_vue_satellite_du_golfe_persique.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/PersianGulf_vue_satellite_du_golfe_persique.jpg/500px-PersianGulf_vue_satellite_du_golfe_persique.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Persian Gulf from space.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APersianGulf_vue_satellite_du_golfe_persique.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/A_Joassamee_Dow_in_full_chase.jpg/500px-A_Joassamee_Dow_in_full_chase.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/A_Joassamee_Dow_in_full_chase.jpg/500px-A_Joassamee_Dow_in_full_chase.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An Al Qasimi dhow in full chase.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AA_Joassamee_Dow_in_full_chase.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Piratecoastpersiangulf.jpg/500px-Piratecoastpersiangulf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Piratecoastpersiangulf.jpg/500px-Piratecoastpersiangulf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An 1849 map of Arabia denoting the Pirate Coast. Shortly after the map was created, The Pirate Coast was renamed The Trucial Coast (present-day United Arab Emirates).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APiratecoastpersiangulf.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/RahmahIbnJabir.jpg/250px-RahmahIbnJabir.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a1/RahmahIbnJabir.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/RahmahIbnJabir.jpg/250px-RahmahIbnJabir.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A sketch of Rahmah ibn Jabir al-Jalahimah done in 1836.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARahmahIbnJabir.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/No._5._Rus_ul_Khyma%2C_with_the_attack_of_the_H_C-s_Cruisers_on_the_evening_of_11_November_1809_RMG_PW4797.jpg/500px-No._5._Rus_ul_Khyma%2C_with_the_attack_of_the_H_C-s_Cruisers_on_the_evening_of_11_November_1809_RMG_PW4797.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/No._5._Rus_ul_Khyma%2C_with_the_attack_of_the_H_C-s_Cruisers_on_the_evening_of_11_November_1809_RMG_PW4797.jpg/500px-No._5._Rus_ul_Khyma%2C_with_the_attack_of_the_H_C-s_Cruisers_on_the_evening_of_11_November_1809_RMG_PW4797.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" The strategic port-city of Ras Al Khaimah, the main base for naval operations conducted by Qawasim","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANo._5._Rus_ul_Khyma%2C_with_the_attack_of_the_H_C-s_Cruisers_on_the_evening_of_11_November_1809_RMG_PW4797.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/No._2._Muskat_Harbour_from_Fishermens_Rock_RMG_PW4794.jpg/500px-No._2._Muskat_Harbour_from_Fishermens_Rock_RMG_PW4794.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/No._2._Muskat_Harbour_from_Fishermens_Rock_RMG_PW4794.